WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.212 --> 00:00:02.239
(dramatic piano music)

00:00:02.239 --> 00:00:06.406
- We live in a country that
has incredible abundance.

00:00:07.985 --> 00:00:11.168
But if you were to add
up all of the hungry people,

00:00:11.168 --> 00:00:14.165
in Los Angeles County, and
its contiguous counties

00:00:14.165 --> 00:00:17.057
as well, you'd have a state of hunger

00:00:17.057 --> 00:00:19.754
the size of New Mexico, or West Virginia.

00:00:19.754 --> 00:00:21.707
Two and a half million people.

00:00:21.707 --> 00:00:25.117
To me, it is a mandate for
us to do better around this.

00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:29.117
And it's so simple, it's
just sharing abundance.

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:33.117
(uplifting instrumental music)

00:00:33.117 --> 00:00:34.469
My name's Rick Nahmias, I'm founder

00:00:34.469 --> 00:00:37.358
and Executive Director of Food Forward.

00:00:37.358 --> 00:00:39.482
Our mission is to recover
fresh produce that

00:00:39.482 --> 00:00:41.125
would otherwise go to waste, and get it

00:00:41.125 --> 00:00:42.562
to people in need.

00:00:42.562 --> 00:00:45.449
We started in 2009, and it really was just

00:00:45.449 --> 00:00:46.950
walking around my neighborhood, and seeing

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.358
all these fruit trees
dripping with citrus,

00:00:49.358 --> 00:00:51.403
that I realized that
with a few volunteers,

00:00:51.403 --> 00:00:54.701
there might be a project here
that I could help facilitate.

00:00:54.701 --> 00:00:55.969
Eight and a half years later,

00:00:55.969 --> 00:00:59.386
and 43 million pounds later, here we are.

00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:04.676
(bouncing marimba music)

00:01:04.676 --> 00:01:08.930
We have fed over one and a
half million people this year.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:10.738
We have three programs; backyard harvest,

00:01:10.738 --> 00:01:12.846
literally going into folks' homes

00:01:12.846 --> 00:01:14.724
who have agreed to have us in there,

00:01:14.724 --> 00:01:16.770
taking excess produce from their trees,

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.570
sometimes their gardens.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:20.895
The second program that was added a couple

00:01:20.895 --> 00:01:24.103
years later was Farmer's Market Recovery.

00:01:24.103 --> 00:01:26.665
We're at about 24 Farmer's
Markets every single week

00:01:26.665 --> 00:01:28.415
with volunteer teams.

00:01:29.786 --> 00:01:31.676
And then the big one is
the wholesale recovery,

00:01:31.676 --> 00:01:35.652
which works kind of
under cloak or darkness.

00:01:35.652 --> 00:01:37.977
We get up super early, usually before

00:01:37.977 --> 00:01:40.194
the sun even comes up, and are out there

00:01:40.194 --> 00:01:41.739
with our trucks gathering pallets,

00:01:41.739 --> 00:01:44.648
and entire truckloads of food that is

00:01:44.648 --> 00:01:46.788
perfectly viable, but for whatever reason

00:01:46.788 --> 00:01:50.151
would otherwise end up in a trash dump.

00:01:50.151 --> 00:01:51.414
We're looking at things that just might

00:01:51.414 --> 00:01:55.128
have a slight discoloration,
or a slight bruise on them.

00:01:55.128 --> 00:01:58.823
It still has weeks left to its
life, but it's not perfect.

00:01:58.823 --> 00:02:01.406
And that's where we get called.

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:05.764
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:02:05.764 --> 00:02:08.779
We have learned that
hunger hides in plain sight

00:02:08.779 --> 00:02:10.830
here in society.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.713
There should be no shame around it.

00:02:12.713 --> 00:02:15.081
Unfortunately there is, but the more that

00:02:15.081 --> 00:02:17.865
we bring abundance out,
and really give people

00:02:17.865 --> 00:02:19.731
food with dignity, and give them quality,

00:02:19.731 --> 00:02:22.825
healthy, nutritious food, these issues

00:02:22.825 --> 00:02:24.212
will slowly go away.

00:02:24.212 --> 00:02:27.879
(dramatic orchestral music)

